  "chan-chi-hwa": {
    "image": "",
    "imageAlt": "",
    "bio": "Chan Chi-hwa is a Hong Kong martial-arts director also credited as Chen Chi-hwa and Charlie Chen. He became active during the period when Hong Kong kung-fu cinema was reaching a large international audience. His directing credits include Shaolin Wooden Men, Snake & Crane Arts of Shaolin, and Half a Loaf of Kung Fu. Several of those films feature Jackie Chan before his emergence as a global star. Chan Chi-hwa also worked on productions that blended traditional martial-arts plots with comedy and fantasy. His filmography represents an important part of Jackie Chan's early screen development and the broader Lo Wei production era.",
    "credit": ""
  },
  "chan-chuen": {
    "image": "",
    "imageAlt": "",
    "bio": "Chan Chuen is a Hong Kong filmmaker associated with martial-arts cinema of the late 1970s and early 1980s. His work often combines kung-fu action with broad comedy and fast-moving adventure plots. He directed Kung Fu vs. Yoga, a film built around a series of unusual martial-arts challenges. His credits also include Two Fists Against the Law. Chan later directed Fearless Hyena Part II, a sequel assembled around Jackie Chan's earlier footage and new material. His films belong to the highly inventive low-budget action tradition that flourished in Hong Kong during this period.",
    "credit": ""
  },
